damage without waiting for a projectile to arrive. In a projectile game, a wallbang would require the bullet to physically fly up to the wall, penetrate it (maybe spawn fragments inside), and continue moving—all over multiple frames, with the network having to keep that in sync. That’s a much harder problem, which is why many games use hitscan for most firearms even today. --- **In summary:** CS:GO does indeed trace a bullet path through walls, with thickness-based damage loss, all in a single raycast. That’s “path tracking” in a geometric sense, but not “trajectory tracking” in the sense of a moving, simulated bullet. The bullet has no travel time, no physics, and no existence beyond the instant of firing. That’s the core of hitscan—and it’s exactly what allows wallbangs to work without adding any network or simulation overhead.
