12:30:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right to zoom out and ask what any of this technical hair-splitting actually means when you’re just trying to have fun playing the game. The honest answer is: **for 95% of what you see and feel, it doesn’t matter that the bullet was a ray and not a moving object.** But there *are* a handful of very tangible, gameplay‑shaping differences that you absolutely experience as a player, even if you never think about the code underneath. That’s where the distinction earns its keep. Let me translate the technical stuff into what you actually feel with a controller or mouse in your hands. --- ### 1. The biggest player‑facing difference: **Can you dodge a bullet?** - **In a hitscan game (CS:GO, Valorant, CoD, Siege)**: Once the trigger is pulled, the damage is decided *in that same split‑second*. You cannot react to a gunshot and move out of the way. If you were in their crosshair when they clicked, you get hit – even if you’re already sidestepping behind a wall on your own screen (due to network lag, they might have seen you still exposed). - **In a projectile‑based game (PUBG, Battlefield, Apex)**: Bullets have travel time. At range, you can literally see the shot coming and dodge it. You hear the crack, see the dust kick up, and move. That’s why leading your target is a core skill, and why sniper duels feel so different between the two types. This single difference changes how you peek corners, how you move, and how you think about engagement distances. In hitscan, positioning and pre‑aim are everything because once the shot is fired it’s too late to react. In projectile games, movement *while* the bullet is in flight matters just as much. --- ### 2. Peeker’s advantage and being shot around corners This is the one most players rage about without knowing why. In a **hitscan** game, thanks to lag compensation, the shooter sees you where you were 50 ms ago. If they click on your head in that old position, the server will often validate it. So you might die *after* you’ve already ducked behind a wall on your screen. You feel robbed because the game “tracked” you through the wall, but that’s just the instant ray hitting the lag‑compensated hitbox. The visual died‑behind‑cover experience is a direct consequence of the hitscan model combined with latency. In a **projectile** game, if you duck behind cover before the bullet arrives, you’re safe. The bullet still has to physically travel to where you were, and if your hitbox isn’t there when the bullet gets there, you don’t get hit. This feels fairer to many players, but it also means you have more time to react and the netcode focuses on where the bullet is *now*, not where you were when it was fired. So the “why did I die behind a wall?” moment is far more common and pronounced in hitscan titles, and it’s a direct player‑experience result of the instant‑ray model. --- ### 3. How weapons *feel* to shoot - **Hitscan guns feel snappy and immediate.** You click, and the hit‑marker appears instantly. There’s no need to lead a target at any range; you point at the head and fire. This makes snaps, flicks, and twitch aiming dominant. It’s satisfying in a very crisp, responsive way. - **Projectile guns feel weighty and require prediction.** You have to aim ahead of a moving target, and the farther they are, the more you lead. Bullet drop adds a lobbing feel. The gratification is delayed – you fire, wait, then see the impact. This makes long‑range engagements more skill‑based and less about pure flick reflexes. Even if both show a tracer, the *tactile* feedback loop is different. In hitscan you’re rewarded instantly for accuracy; in projectile you’re rewarded for prediction. That changes the entire rhythm of a gunfight. --- ### 4. Penetration through walls behaves differently In CS:GO, wallbang damage is calculated in a single step: the ray enters the wall, loses X damage per unit thickness, and whatever is left continues. The bullet doesn’t slow down over time; it loses damage based purely on *material distance traversed*. So a thin wooden door reduces your damage by a flat amount, and if there’s enough left, it instantly hits the player behind. In a projectile game like PUBG, the bullet physically passes through the wall, losing velocity and damage as it does so. The slower exit velocity means more drop after penetration and a slightly longer flight time. That’s a simulation quirk you might never notice, but it makes wallbangs slightly harder to predict and more organic. --- ### Do you, the player, need to know this? Not actively, no. You don’t pull up an M4 in CS:GO and think “Ah, this is a hitscan weapon, therefore I must…” – you just play. But the *designers* chose hitscan or projectile to craft a specific experience, and that choice shapes the entire feel of the game. The fact that the game “always knows the bullet’s path” is true in both cases (and that’s what you correctly zeroed in on). The part that changes your experience isn’t that the path is *recorded* – it’s **when** the path is resolved (instantly vs. over time) and **what can happen during that time**. So yes, the distinction does matter to you, the regular player, not as a piece of trivia, but as the invisible force that decides: - Whether you die behind a wall - Whether you can dodge a sniper shot you see coming - Whether you need to lead your target or just click on their head You’ve been feeling those differences every time you switch between a game like CS:GO and PUBG, even if you didn’t have words for it. That’s why we tech devs care about the distinction – because it lets us tune exactly how the game feels to you, the person holding the mouse. And you’re absolutely right that under the hood, both are just “finding the bullet’s path”; but the *when* and *how long* that path exists are what create two completely different kinds of shooter.
